---
// Section 8 of the 2024 election autopsy: how the "slope" in chart 08 is
// calculated. Method from the analysis repo's report/preregistration_2026_general.md
// (Test A) and its addendum 1. The formula is MathML, which current browsers render natively.
---

<aside class="formula" aria-labelledby="slope-formula-title">
  <p class="label" id="slope-formula-title">How the slope is calculated</p>
  <p>For each Michigan city or township <var>i</var>:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>gap<sub>i</sub></strong> = El-Sayed’s share − Benson’s share, in points, where each share is the Democrat’s votes ÷ (Democratic + Republican votes)</li>
    <li><strong>swing<sub>i</sub></strong> = Trump’s share in 2024 − Trump’s share in 2020, measured the same way</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Fit a straight line, gap = <var>a</var> + <var>b</var> × swing, through all the towns, with bigger towns counting
    for more (each is weighted by its votes cast, <var>w<sub>i</sub></var>). Towns with fewer than 200 votes are
    left out. The slope is
  </p>
  <div class="math-scroll">
    <math display="block">
      <mi>b</mi>
      <mo>=</mo>
      <mfrac>
        <mrow>
          <munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder>
          <msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub>
          <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
          <msub><mtext>swing</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub>
          <mo>−</mo>
          <mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover>
          <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
          <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
          <msub><mtext>gap</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub>
          <mo>−</mo>
          <mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover>
          <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          <munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder>
          <msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub>
          <msup>
            <mrow>
              <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
              <msub><mtext>swing</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub>
              <mo>−</mo>
              <mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover>
              <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
            </mrow>
            <mn>2</mn>
          </msup>
        </mrow>
      </mfrac>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    where <var>s̄</var> and <var>ḡ</var> are the vote-weighted averages of swing and gap across all the towns. The
    whiskers on the chart are 95% confidence intervals for <var>b</var>, using standard errors that don’t assume every
    town is equally noisy.
  </p>
  <p>
    The stricter version fits the same kind of line but also includes each town’s earlier swings (2008–12, 2012–16,
    2016–20), its 2020 Republican share, four census measures and a term for each county; <var>b</var> is then the
    swing’s slope with all of those held fixed.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Reading it.</strong> <var>b</var> = +0.1 means that for every extra point a town swung to Trump,
    El-Sayed ran 0.1 points further ahead of Benson. So a town that swung 10 points would have him 1 point further
    ahead than a town that didn’t swing at all. <var>b</var> = 0 means his gap to Benson is the same everywhere,
    whichever way the town moved in 2024.
  </p>
</aside>

